EXT. BRIDGE OF MOSS - TWILIGHT

A stone bridge arches over a stream that runs upward, against gravity, into a pink sky. Lanterns drift like soap bubbles.

EIRWEN, 30s, a ferrywoman with silver-streaked braids, sits on the parapet mending a net. BRAM, 20s, in a tunic too thin for the evening, stands at the near end with a satchel. OLD NETTLE, 80s, a toad-faced hermit, squats on the far end and eats a pear.

Bram steps onto the bridge. The stones hum under his boot.

EIRWEN
You are early.

BRAM
I walked all night.

EIRWEN
You are still early.

He reaches the middle. Nettle chews.

OLD NETTLE
Toll.

Bram opens the satchel. Takes out a small bell. Holds it out. Nettle looks at it without touching it.

OLD NETTLE (CONT'D)
That's not what she took last time.

EIRWEN
Last time was not him.

Bram lowers the bell. The stream runs up through the air behind them.

BRAM
Is it enough?

Eirwen finishes a knot. Bites the thread.

EIRWEN
Ask again at dawn.
